  "pattern": "off-by-one",
  "difficulty_rating": 1100,
  "explanation": "The initial window-sum loop uses `i <= k` instead of `i < k`, so it runs 4 times (i = 0,1,2,3) instead of 3, summing k+1 = 4 elements (2+1+5+1 = 9) into what should be a 3-element window. That inflated starting sum then gets carried through every subsequent slide (`windowSum += arr[i] - arr[i - k]`), which correctly swaps one element in and one out but is now operating on a window that's permanently one element too wide in composition. The correct maximum 3-element window sum is 9 (from [5,1,3]), but the bug produces a reported max of 10 because the corrupted window sums happen to peak higher at index 4.",
  "prompt": "Step through this fixed-window max-sum function and predict the final printed value.",
  "language": "javascript",
  "snippet": "function maxSumSubarray(arr, k) {\n  let windowSum = 0;\n  for (let i = 0; i <= k; i++) {\n    windowSum += arr[i];\n  }\n  let maxSum = windowSum;\n  console.log(\"initial window sum:\", windowSum);\n\n  for (let i = k; i < arr.length; i++) {\n    windowSum += arr[i] - arr[i - k];\n    maxSum = Math.max(maxSum, windowSum);\n    console.log(\"window ending at index\", i, \"sum:\", windowSum);\n  }\n\n  console.log(\"max sum:\", maxSum);\n  return maxSum;\n}\n\nmaxSumSubarray([2, 1, 5, 1, 3, 2], 3);",
